message:
I wrote a function that does this, but it's not in C.  I am in the process
of converting it to C, but I don't know how long it will take.  My Pascal
description follows below.  If someone wants to beat me to it, I'd be
happy to send you the Pascal code.

The algorithm is a modified binary search (which is why it's so fast).
The idea is to choose a "perfect" value as close as we can get to the
actual 24-bit color.  The problem is that now we have to find the closest
match given a 16-bit color space.  The technique used is to add the
difference between the original color and the 16-bit color to the
original color, so that the next time through the loop, we can look for
a better match.  If the new color is worse, we use the old color.
In this way, we converge to a color that is close to the original color.

I do the search not on the actual RGB values, but on the hue, saturation
and brightness of the colors.  This gives a much more accurate match
than straight RGB values.  The code below is not in C, but is in
Microsoft Pascal.  It should be quite easy to convert to C.

  FUNCTION RGBColorToIndexedColor (Color : RGBColor) : INTEGER;

    (* Converts RGB format into the closest palette entry.  This is a
       modified binary search routine.  *)

    VAR
      (* The first color we start with.  It is the closest color in the
         16-bit color space to the actual color. *)

      CurrentColor   : RGBColor;
      Index          : INTEGER;
      Temp           : RGBColor;
      Temp2          : RGBColor;
      Hue            : REAL;
      Saturation     : REAL;
      Brightness     : REAL;
      HueDifference  : REAL;
      SaturationDifference  : REAL;
      BrightnessDifference  : REAL;
      OldDifference  : REAL;
      NewDifference  : REAL;

    BEGIN
      (* Convert the Color to HSB values. *)

      ColorToHSB (Color, Hue, Saturation, Brightness);

      (* Convert the values to 16-bit values. *)

      CurrentColor.Red   := TRUNC (Color.Red   / 8);
      CurrentColor.Green := TRUNC (Color.Green / 8);
      CurrentColor.Blue  := TRUNC (Color.Blue  / 8);

      (* Get the index of the color. *)

      Index := INDEXVALUE (CurrentColor);

      (* Get the real RGB values for the 16-bit color. *)

      Temp := PALETTE [Index];

      (* Convert to HSB values. *)

      ColorToHSB (Temp, HueDifference, SaturationDifference, BrightnessDifference);

      (* Calculate the difference between the two colors. *)

      OldDifference := ABS (Hue - HueDifference) + ABS (Saturation - SaturationDifference) +
                       ABS (Brightness - BrightnessDifference);

      (* Loop until we converge on a color.  This is a modified binary
         search. *)

      REPEAT
        (* Create a new guess for the color. *)

        Temp2.Red   := TRUNC (Color.Red   / 8) + (Color.Red   - (Temp.Red   * 8));
        Temp2.Green := TRUNC (Color.Green / 8) + (Color.Green - (Temp.Green * 8));
        Temp2.Blue  := TRUNC (Color.Blue  / 8) + (Color.Blue  - (Temp.Blue  * 8));

        (* Get the index of the new color. *)

        Temp := PALETTE [INDEXVALUE (Temp2)];

        (* Convert to HSB values. *)

        ColorToHSB (Temp, HueDifference, SaturationDifference, BrightnessDifference);

        (* Calculate the difference between the two colors. *)

        NewDifference := ABS (Hue - HueDifference) + ABS (Saturation - SaturationDifference) +
                         ABS (Brightness - BrightnessDifference);

        (* If the new color is better, use it.  Otherwise, use the old
           color. *)

        IF NewDifference < OldDifference THEN
        BEGIN
          OldDifference := NewDifference;
          CurrentColor  := Temp2;
        END;

      UNTIL (NewDifference >= OldDifference);

      (* Return the index of the color. *)

      RGBColorToIndexedColor := INDEXVALUE (CurrentColor);

    END;
